$(
=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
        Abian's "most fundamental" fixed point theorem
=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
$)

  ${
    $d x y v A $.  $d x y z w v u F $.  $d y v u G $.
    abianfp.1 $e |- A e. V $.
    abianfp.2 $e |- G = rec ( { <. z , w >. | w = ( F ` z ) } , x ) $.
    $( Lemma for ~ abianfp .  We prove by transfinite induction that if ` F `
       has a fixed point ` x ` , then its iterates also equal ` x ` .  This
       lemma is used for the "trivial" direction of the main theorem. $)
    abianfplem $p |- ( v e. On -> ( ( F ` x ) = x -> ( G ` v ) = x ) ) $=
      ( vy vu cv cfv wceq c0 csuc fveq2 eqeq1d copab crdg fveq1i visset rdg0
      eqtri a1i con0 wcel wa cvv fvex ax-17 hbopab1 hbrdg hbxfr hbfv
      rdgsucopab mpan2 id sylan9eqr sylan9eq exp32 wlim wral wi ciun rdglim2a
      mpan iuneq2i 3eqtr4g adantr iuneq2 wne word cuni w3a df-lim 3simp2 sylbi
      iunconst syl eqtrd ex a1d tfinds2 ) DLZGMZALZNZOGMZWGNZJLZGMZWGNZWKPZGMZW
      GNZWGFMZWGNZDJWEONWFWIWGWEOGQRWEWKNWFWLWGWEWKGQRWEWNNWFWOWGWEWNGQRWJWRWIO
      CLBLZFMZNZBCSZWGTZMWGOGXCIUAWGXBAUBUCUDUEWKUFUGZWRWMWPXDWRWMUHWOWLFMZWGXD
      XEUIUGWOXENWLFUJBCKWGWKWTXEUIGKLZWGUGBUKZXFWKUGBUKZBKWLFXFFUGBUKBKWKGBKGX
      CIBKWGXBXABCKULXGUMUNXHUOUOIWSWLFQUPUQWMWRXEWQWGWLWGFQWRURUSUTVAWEVBZWMJW
      EVCZWHVDWRXIXJWHXIXJUHWFJWEWLVEZWGXIWFXKNXJXIWEXCMZJWEWKXCMZVEZWFXKWEUIUG
      XIXLXNNDUBJWGWEUIXBVFVGWEGXCIUAJWEWLXMWLXMNWKWEUGWKGXCIUAUEVHVIVJXJXIXKJW
      EWGVEZWGJWEWLWGVKXIWEOVLZXOWGNXIWEVMZXPWEWEVNNZVOXPWEVPXQXPXRVQVRJWEWGVSV
      TUSWAWBWCWD $.
      $( [7-Sep-2004] $) $( [4-Sep-2004] $)

    $( No longer active:
       ~ http://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~~suh/abian/abian-themostfixed.html . $)
    $( "A most fundamental fixed point theorem" of Alexander Abian (1923-1999),
       apparently proved in 1998.  Let ` G `` 0 = x ` , ` G `` 1 = F `` x ` ,
       ` G `` 2 = F `` ( F `` x ) ` ,... be the iterates of ` F ` .  The
       theorem reads (using our variable names):  "Let ` F ` be a mapping from
       a set ` A ` into itself.  Then ` F ` has a fixed point if and only if:
       There exists an element ` x ` of ` A ` such that for every ordinal ` v `
       , ` G `` v ` is an element of ` A ` , and if ` G `` v ` is not a fixed
       point of ` F ` then the ` G `` u ` 's are all distinct for every ordinal
       ` u e. v ` ." See ~ df-rdg for the ` rec ` operation.  The proof's key
       idea is to assume that ` F ` does not have a fixed point, then use the
       Axiom of Replacement in the form of ~ f1dmex to derive that the class of
       all ordinal numbers exists, contradicting ~ onprc .  Our version of this
       theorem does not require the hypothesis that ` F ` be a mapping.
       Reference: ~ http://us2.metamath.org:88/abian-themostfixed.html .
       For an application of this theorem, see
       ~ http://groups.google.com/group/sci.stat.math/msg/1737ee1133c24aeb
       for its use in a proof of Tarski's fixed point theorem. $)
    abianfp $p |- ( E. x e. A ( F ` x ) = x <-> E. x e. A
           A. v e. On ( ( G ` v ) e. A /\ ( -. ( F ` ( G ` v ) ) = ( G ` v ) ->
                    A. u e. v -. ( G ` v ) = ( G ` u ) ) ) ) $=
      ( vy cv cfv wceq wrex wcel wn wral wi wa con0 abianfplem imp eleq1d
      biimprd fveq2 id eqeq12d biimprcd sylcom pm2.24d jctird ex com13
      r19.21adv r19.22i cvv onprc r19.26 notbid rcla4cv imim1d r19.20sdv
      r19.20 syl6 com12 wf1 wf ccnv wfun wfn copab crdg rdgfnon wb fneq1 ax-mp
      mpbir ffnfv biimpri mpan cres wss ssid tz7.48lem fnresdm cnveqi funeq
      sylib anim12i df-f1 sylibr f1dmex mpan2 syl syld exp3a com23 sylbi mtoi
      a1i r19.23aiv cbvrexv dfrex2 bitr2i impbii ) ALZGMZXGNZAFOZDLZHMZFPZXLGMZ
      XLNZQZXLELHMNQEXKRZSZTZDUARZAFOZXIXTAFXGFPZXIXSDUAXKUAPZXIYBXSYCXIYBXSSYC
      XITZYBXMXRYDXMYBYDXLXGFYCXIXLXGNZABCDFGHIJUBZUCUDUEYDXOXQYCXIXOYCXIYEXOYF
      YEXOXIYEXNXHXLXGXLXGGUFYEUGUHUIUJUCUKULUMUNUOUPYAKLZGMZYGNZQZKFRZQZXJXTYL
      AFXTYLSYBXTYKUAUQPZURXTXMDUARZXRDUARZTYKYMSZXMXRDUAUSYNYOYPYNYKYOYMYNYKYO
