  $c prod_ $. $( Product sign. $)

  $( Extend class notation to include finite products. $)
  cprd $a class prod_ k e. A G B $.

  ${
    $d m n x y k $. $d m n x y A $.  $d m n x y G $.  $d m n x y B $.
    $( Define   the  finite  recursive  operation  associated  to  the
       operation `  G ` .  It  may be thought  as a product, a  sum or
       whatever.  ` A  ` is the set  of indices, ` G `  is an internal
       operation, and ` B ` is an expression (  typically  referencing
       ` k ` ). This corresponds to the first part of the definition of
       ~  df-sum .   The operation  `  + `  has been  replaced by  the
       generic  operation ` G  ` .   The reference  to the  concept of
       limit has been removed because  one wants to use the product in
       contexts where  limits are irrelevant. One could  be still more
       generic and replace ` ( m ... n ) ` by a finite totally ordered
       set. We would then get  the definition given by Bourbaki in the
       first chapter  of the  algebra book of  his treatise
       ( A I.3 def.4 ).   I don't
       specify anything  about ` G  ` because  nothing is required  of
       ` g ` in the definition of ` seq ` .  I hope it will be ok.
       Otherwise we could add ` G e. Magma ` . $)
    df-prod $a |- prod_ k e. A G B = { x | E. m E. n e. ( ZZ>= ` m )
        ( A = ( m ... n ) /\
      x e. ( ( <. m , G >. seq ( { <. k , y >. | y = B } |` ZZ ) ) ` n ) ) } $.
  $}

